.TH SUPERVISE 1
.SH NAME
supervise \- keep a set of commands running
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B supervise
[
.B -v
]
[
.B -n
.I runs
]
[
.B -m
.I secs
]
.I command ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Supervise
starts each
.I command
with
.IR sh (1)
and starts it again whenever it ends, for any reason.  Each argument is
one service and is a complete shell command line, so it may be quoted
and may contain several commands.
.PP
The options are
.TP
.B -v
Report each start and each exit on standard error.
.TP
.BI -n " runs"
Start each service at most
.I runs
times and exit when every service has reached that limit.  The default
is no limit, which never returns.
.TP
.BI -m " secs"
Treat a service that lasted fewer than
.I secs
seconds as flapping and wait a second before starting it again.  The
default is 1.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/supervise.b
.SH "HOW IT WORKS"
.I Supervise
opens its own
.B /prog/ pid /wait
before it spawns anything and then blocks reading it.
.IR dis (6)'s
.B newprog
gives every new process a pointer to its parent's wait queue, and the
message posted when a process ends is
.PP
.EX
	pid "module":cause
.EE
.PP
with
.I cause
.B exited
for an ordinary return and otherwise the exception that ended it.
Nothing is polled, and no kernel change was needed for any of this:
.IR prog (3)
already exposes every process as files.
.PP
Only direct children appear.  A grandchild posts to its own parent's
queue, which is empty unless that parent also opened its wait file, so
.I supervise
is told about the things it started and not about everything on the
machine.  One consequence is worth knowing: a service written as a
shell command line is supervised as the shell running it.  If that
shell's own child is killed, the shell ends and the service is
restarted, but the
.B cause
reported is the shell's
.RB ( exited ),
not the child's
.RB ( killed ).
.SH EXAMPLES
Keep a file server and a listener up for as long as the machine runs:
.IP
.EX
supervise -v 'styxlisten -A tcp!*!6666 export /' 'cloudfs /n/cloud'
.EE
.PP
Run something three times and stop:
.IP
.EX
supervise -n 3 'echo hello'
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sh (1),
.IR ps (1),
.IR kill (1),
.IR prog (3)
.SH BUGS
A service is restarted immediately unless it ended sooner than
.B -m
allows, and the hold-off is a flat second rather than a growing one.  A
service that fails slowly and forever is restarted forever.
.PP
There is no way to stop or restart one service without stopping
.IR supervise ,
and no control file of its own.
